WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.540
Robert Downey, Jr. has been a fixture in Hollywood
longer than many of his fans have been alive.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.070
While his talent and charisma have always
been undeniable, he was long plagued by offscreen

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.040
troubles that threatened to sink his career.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:16.460
But over the last decade, he's experienced
a personal and professional resurgence that

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.120
has him on top of the world and Hollywood's
A-List.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.720
It's not easy to go from high school dropout
to founding member of the Avengers, but showbiz

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.100
and struggle have been in Downey's blood from
the beginning.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:27.880
Underground dad

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.800
Downey's irreverence, self-deprecating humor,
and anti-authoritarian streak make a lot of

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.480
sense if you're familiar with his father.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.520
Robert Downey, Sr. started writing and directing
independent films in the early 1960s.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.170
His first film to receive a wide release was
the 1969 satire Putney Swope, the story of

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.440
a black man accidentally put in charge of
an advertising agency.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.420
Junior made his film debut as a 5-year-old
in his dad's 1970 movie Pound, in which all

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.780
the actors play dogs.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.440
"That tornado scared me so much, it made my
hair disappear."

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.940
"Have any hair on your balls?"

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.840
A year later, Downey, Sr. let his 6-year-old
son try marijuana for the first time, which

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.500
Downey Sr. later came to greatly regret.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.760
When his parents divorced in 1978, a 13-year
old Junior followed his dad from New York

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:14.260
to L.A.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.260
At the age of 16, he dropped out of high school
to pursue acting full-time.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:19.840
New York beginnings

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.200
Rather than attempt to break into Hollywood,
the teenage Downey returned to his mother's

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:24.780
in New York.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.260
He landed roles in a couple of small productions,
including 1984's Firstborn, a little-remembered

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:33.200
drama co-starring an 18-year-old Sarah Jessica
Parker.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.280
The two hit it off immediately, beginning
a relationship that would last for years even

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.760
as Downey became a mainstay on the New York
party scene.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.280
In 1985, Downey joined the cast of Saturday
Night Live.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.240
Producer Lorne Michaels had just returned
to the show after five years away, and he

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.100
hired an entirely new cast.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.320
It became painfully apparent that Downey was
out of his element.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.380
"That simply represents a dramatic synthesis
of the..."

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.960
Much of the new cast failed to click with
each other, and Downey was among those who

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.220
were canned at the end of the season.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.840
Professional success, personal troubles

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.780
The sacking from SNL was a blessing in disguise,
as it coincided with the rise of Downey's

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:13.560
film career.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.819
He had small roles in the John Hughes films
Weird Science and Pretty in Pink, as well

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.300
as the Rodney Dangerfield comedy Back to School.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:25.319
1987 brought two highlights: his first lead
role in The Pick-Up Artist, and Less Than

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.040
Zero, his most critically acclaimed performance
to date.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.180
Less Than Zero featured Downey as the hard-partying
cocaine addict Julian Wells.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:37.040
It earned him recognition as a serious actor,
but the experience of making it had some very

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.600
unfortunate repercussions.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.940
Drugs had virtually always been a part of
Downey's life, but he's pointed to Less Than

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.280
Zero as the time when the troubles really
began.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:46.720
He explained to The Guardian:

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.340
"The character was an exaggeration of myself.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.820
Then things changed and, in some ways, I became
an exaggeration of the character.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.240
That lasted far longer than it needed to last."

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:57.540
The A-List

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.240
The 1992 bio-pic Chaplin cemented Downey's
reputation as a critically acclaimed actor.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.340
His performance as screen legend Charlie Chaplin
made him one of the most in-demand stars in

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:10.160
Hollywood and earned him a Best Actor Oscar
nomination.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:15.230
He followed that up with acclaimed turns in
Robert Altman's Short Cuts in 1993 and Oliver

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.920
Stone's Natural Born Killers in 1994.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.180
His personal life was also looking up, as
he married actress Deborah Falconer in 1992,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:24.580
and they had a son two years later.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:27.260
But his troubles with addiction would soon
boil over.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.980
Nearly losing it all

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.680
In June 1996, Downey was arrested for felony
cocaine and heroin possession after being

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.600
pulled over for driving while completely naked
in Malibu.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:42.560
A few weeks later, an extremely confused neighbor
found the actor cozily tucked into bed in

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.460
one of his guest rooms.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:48.640
Numerous trips to rehab seemed to do nothing
to quell his habit, and his film roles diminished

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.180
in size and frequency as the arrests piled
up.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.000
A brief comeback on the hit TV series Ally
McBeal was derailed by more drug charges in

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:56.720
2001.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.200
He appeared on The Oprah Winfrey Show in 2004
and spoke about his struggles:

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:06.620
"It's not that difficult to overcome these
seemingly ghastly problems."

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.680
“You are saying that it’s not that difficult?”

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:09.180
“No.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.340
What's hard is to decide.”

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.120
Cleaning up his act

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.360
Downey's marriage to Falconer ended in divorce
in 2004, but he had just met producer Susan

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.220
Levin the year before on the set of Gothika,
and things turned serious pretty quickly.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.820
But Levin let it be known that she wouldn't
marry Downey unless he gave up drugs completely.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:30.000
This was the final nail in the coffin for
his old life, and the two married in 2005.

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:33.880
With a newfound focus and sobriety came an
uptick in the number of interesting roles

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:34.640
offered to Downey.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.080
Well-received turns in Kiss Kiss Bang Bang,
Good Night and Good Luck, and A Scanner Darkly

00:04:39.099 --> 00:04:43.860
culminated in a supporting role in the 2007
thriller Zodiac, one of the most acclaimed

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:44.860
films of that year.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.539
The role of a lifetime

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:51.800
2008's Iron Man has been called, in retrospect,
the riskiest film in history.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.770
By the time of its release, Marvel had been
planning that all of its future films would

00:04:55.770 --> 00:05:00.159
take place in the same shared universe, with
characters crossing over between films.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.520
Iron Man set the tone for this universe, essentially
selling the whole concept of the MCU to filmgoers.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:10.159
Much of this depended upon its leading man,
and if the film had flopped, Downey's career

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.000
might have recovered, but Marvel likely never
would have.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:17.520
But of course, the film was a smash hit with
critics and audiences alike, and among all

00:05:17.539 --> 00:05:21.309
of the praise, Downey was singled out for
inhabiting his character in a way that few

00:05:21.309 --> 00:05:24.000
superhero movie leads have done before or
since.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:29.150
The MCU has gone on to become the highest-grossing
film franchise of all time, and Downey has

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:30.780
been its face from the very beginning.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.700
While he's also continued to snag an Oscar
nomination for 2008's Tropic Thunder and to

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:40.039
create a popular new spin on Sherlock Holmes,
it's become increasingly difficult to tell

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.220
where Downey ends and Stark begins.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:51.020
"The truth is...I 
am Iron Man."

